  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the presence or absence of a sure (certain) outcome in each lottery. However, neither lottery has a sure outcome, so the reason would either be indifferent or — if strictly preferring lotteries without sure outcomes — apply equally to both. The individual does not mention certainty, guaranteed results, or the concept of sure outcomes at all. Instead, their justification centers on risk preference, subjective likelihood neglect ('won’t happen anyway'), and a trade-off between probability and payoff magnitude. There is no indication that the individual considered whether one of the lotteries offered a guaranteed result. The information considered by the individual (probabilities, payoffs, risk) does not align with the information relevant to the decision reason (presence of sure outcomes). Similarly, the justification based on risk-taking for higher reward does not match the rule of preferring lotteries without sure outcomes. Therefore, there is no evidence the individual used this decision reason.",
